,58,18,20,59,50853,19,15,28,16,19,15,15,7110,60,9885,1045,7546,400,77,3,448,400,15,59,273,80,308,27,16,18,24,24,3,1741,429,400,77,3,374,279,12557,65266,27949,311,220,16,21,15,59864,220,16,18,24,24,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nNotice that  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0e14dcac73cf2d5f2613b6327a479b2d836f845b6c37d4f9ab49942cce215068"},"seconds":61.443591833114624,"seed":507544976179589968,"stop":"eos","text":"To find an integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis involves finding an integer \\( n \\) such that the equation holds true. We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the multiplicative inverse.\n\n### Step 1: Apply the Euclidean Algorithm\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 160 \\cdot 8 + 119 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 119 \\cdot 1 + 41 \\)\n3. \\( 119 = 41 \\cdot 2 + 37 \\)\n4. \\( 41 = 37 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\)\n5. \\( 37 = 4 \\cdot 9 + 1 \\)\n6. \\( 4 = 4 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, which confirms that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and thus an inverse exists.\n\n### Step 2: Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm\nNext, we backtrack through the steps of the Euclidean algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nFrom step 5:\n\\[ 1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot 4 \\]\n\nFrom step 4:\n\\[ 4 = 41 - 37 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 4 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot (41 - 37) = 37 - 9 \\cdot 41 + 9 \\cdot 37 = 10 \\cdot 37 - 9 \\cdot 41 \\]\n\nFrom step 3:\n\\[ 37 = 119 - 2 \\cdot 41 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 37 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 10 \\cdot (119 - 2 \\cdot 41) - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 20 \\cdot 41 - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 41 \\]\n\nFrom step 2:\n\\[ 41 = 160 - 119 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 41 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot (160 - 119) = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160 + 29 \\cdot 119 = 39 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nFrom step 1:\n\\[ 119 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 119 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 39 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 312 \\cdot 160 - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 341 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ 1 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 341 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ -341 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the Positive Equivalent\nSince we need \\( n \\) to be positive and within the range \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\), we convert \\(-341\\) to a positive equivalent:\n\\[ n = -341 + 1399 = 1058 \\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1058} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1058}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find an integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis involves finding an integer \\( n \\) such that the equation holds true. We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the multiplicative inverse.\n\n### Step 1: Apply the Euclidean Algorithm\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 160 \\cdot 8 + 119 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 119 \\cdot 1 + 41 \\)\n3. \\( 119 = 41 \\cdot 2 + 37 \\)\n4. \\( 41 = 37 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\)\n5. \\( 37 = 4 \\cdot 9 + 1 \\)\n6. \\( 4 = 4 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, which confirms that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and thus an inverse exists.\n\n### Step 2: Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm\nNext, we backtrack through the steps of the Euclidean algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nFrom step 5:\n\\[ 1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot 4 \\]\n\nFrom step 4:\n\\[ 4 = 41 - 37 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 4 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot (41 - 37) = 37 - 9 \\cdot 41 + 9 \\cdot 37 = 10 \\cdot 37 - 9 \\cdot 41 \\]\n\nFrom step 3:\n\\[ 37 = 119 - 2 \\cdot 41 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 37 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 10 \\cdot (119 - 2 \\cdot 41) - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 20 \\cdot 41 - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 41 \\]\n\nFrom step 2:\n\\[ 41 = 160 - 119 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 41 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot (160 - 119) = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160 + 29 \\cdot 119 = 39 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nFrom step 1:\n\\[ 119 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\nSubstitute \\( 119 \\) in the previous equation:\n\\[ 1 = 39 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 312 \\cdot 160 - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 341 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ 1 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 341 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ -341 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the Positive Equivalent\nSince we need \\( n \\) to be positive and within the range \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\), we convert \\(-341\\) to a positive equivalent:\n\\[ n = -341 + 1399 = 1058 \\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1058} \\]","id":"number_theory:231"}
